module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.ClosedImmersion
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.FiniteType
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Proper
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Pullbacks
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Limits.Shapes.Pullback.HasPullback
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.ZariskisMainTheorem
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Gluing
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.VectorSpace
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basis

/-! # Closed factorization locus for a constant source

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 1.105, printed pp.37--38,
Example 1.100 and Proposition 1.101, printed p.36
(blueprint: lem-sfb-constant-source-closed-factorization-locus).

The ideal-sheaf encoding makes uniqueness equality of closed subschemes. Unique lifts
identify the condition with the represented subfunctor of the functor of points of S.
Every map to S gives its source the induced k-scheme structure; thus the tests below
range over all k-schemes and all k-morphisms to S, without finiteness restrictions.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The closed locus on which a constant-source family factors through a closed immersion,
with its universal property after every base change. All factorizations are scheme
morphisms; their displayed equations force them to be morphisms over k. The unique-lift
property identifies Hom(T,L) with the indicated subset of Hom(T,S), naturally under
precomposition. Hence it gives the stated representing subfunctor, retaining nilpotents.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 1.105, printed pp.37--38,
Example 1.100 and Proposition 1.101, printed p.36
(blueprint: lem-sfb-constant-source-closed-factorization-locus).

Proof plan:
1. Pull back W along f. The resulting closed immersion D into Y times S is a
   monomorphism (lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-base-change and
   lem-pc-proper-monomorphism-closed). Choose a finite affine cover Spec B_a of Y
   using def-weil-finite-type-morphism. Over Spec A in S the charts are
   Spec(B_a tensor A), and D is defined by ideals K_a, by
   lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra, lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product and
   lem-weil-pbc-closed-immersion-affine-cover-criterion.
2. Choose k-bases of B_a (lem-weil-pbc-vector-space-free). Their base changes are
   bases over every test algebra (lem-weil-pbc-free-module-base-change-free).
   Let J_A be generated by all coordinates of all elements of all K_a. Independence
   of the base-changed bases says that a map A to R kills J_A exactly when every
   K_a maps to zero in B_a tensor R.
3. The quotient universal property and def-weil-affine-spectrum identify that
   vanishing with a lift on each affine chart. Monicity makes these lifts agree;
   lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing glues them. The charts cover the product by
   lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product. Thus the criterion holds for arbitrary test
   algebras, including nonreduced ones.
4. On a principal open A_h, the extended ideal J_A A_h and the freshly constructed
   ideal have the same annihilating maps to every algebra. Testing their quotient
   maps proves both inclusions, so the ideals coincide independently of choices.
5. Apply lem-weil-pbc-affine-ideals-closed-subscheme to this principal-open
   compatibility. The resulting ideal sheaf I defines L and the affine criterion.
6. Use lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis to cover an arbitrary test scheme by affine
   opens mapping into affine opens of S. Monicity and
   lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing extend the criterion to every test scheme;
   lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product supplies the corresponding product cover.
7. Monicity makes every lift unique and the represented map on points injective.
   Precomposition preserves factorization. Two representing closed subschemes map
   uniquely to one another over S by their universal properties; monicity makes
   the composites identities, giving equality of their defining ideal sheaves.
8. For any b : S' to S, the pullback of L is closed. The fibre-product universal
   property identifies lifts of t : T to S' with lifts of t followed by b to L.
   The original criterion gives exactly the base-changed family condition, since
   product maps compose by their projections. No finiteness assumption on S' is used. -/
theorem existsUnique_constantSource_closedFactorizationLocus
    {k : Type u} [Field k] (Y X S : Over (Spec (.of k)))
    [IsSeparated Y.hom] [LocallyOfFiniteType Y.hom] [QuasiCompact Y.hom]
    [IsSeparated X.hom] [LocallyOfFiniteType X.hom] [QuasiCompact X.hom]
    [IsSeparated S.hom] [LocallyOfFiniteType S.hom] [QuasiCompact S.hom]
    (W : Scheme.{u}) (i : W ⟶ X.left) [IsClosedImmersion i]
    (f : pullback Y.hom S.hom ⟶ X.left)
    (hf : f ≫ X.hom = pullback.snd Y.hom S.hom ≫ S.hom) :
    ∃! I : S.left.IdealSheafData,
      (∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (s : T ⟶ S.left),
        (∃! l : T ⟶ I.subscheme, l ≫ I.subschemeι = s) ↔
          ∃ w : pullback Y.hom (s ≫ S.hom) ⟶ W,
            w ≫ i = pullback.map Y.hom (s ≫ S.hom) Y.hom S.hom
              (𝟙 Y.left) s (𝟙 (Spec (.of k))) (by simp) (by simp) ≫ f) ∧
      ∀ (S' : Scheme.{u}) (b : S' ⟶ S.left),
        IsClosedImmersion (pullback.snd I.subschemeι b) ∧
        ∀ (T : Scheme.{u}) (t : T ⟶ S'),
          (∃! l : T ⟶ pullback I.subschemeι b, l ≫ pullback.snd I.subschemeι b = t) ↔
            ∃ w : pullback Y.hom ((t ≫ b) ≫ S.hom) ⟶ W,
              w ≫ i = pullback.map Y.hom ((t ≫ b) ≫ S.hom) Y.hom S.hom
                (𝟙 Y.left) (t ≫ b) (𝟙 (Spec (.of k))) (by simp) (by simp) ≫ f := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
